# Target C: global square-root leaf-fan port / stress-flat fork

**Date:** 2026-10-02  
**Base main:** 61e4238bad5baab3dd4b466a487408b1d037f0dc  
**Status:** proof-only reduction. Level I and C-II remain open.

## 1. Purpose

The current square-root matching branch has two local inputs.

1. research/target_c_leaf_fan_outerwalk_fork_2026-10-02.md proves that if the
   actual-diameter matching number and loaded outer-walk excess are both
   \(O(\sqrt D)\), then \(D-O(\sqrt D)\) loaded-hull contacts lie in exact
   same-carrier diameter-leaf fans.
2. research/target_c_leaf_fan_inward_spoke_kkt_fork_2026-10-02.md proves a
   local fork for one good fan run: many degree-three fan vertices give inward
   third-contact spokes, while many degree-two fan vertices give exact KKT stress
   transport.

The local report extracted only an \(\Omega(D^{1/4})\) object from one
\(\Omega(\sqrt D)\)-long fan. The present note globalizes over all good fan runs.
Because the entire good-fan population is linear while the number of runs is only
\(O(\sqrt D)\), the exponent improves from \(1/4\) to \(1/2\).

No computation is used.

## 2. Setup and exact good-fan population

Let
\[
H=(B,E_H),\qquad h=|E_H|,\qquad \nu=\nu(H),
\]
and let
\[
x_B=b_{\rm out}-q
\]
be the loaded outer-walk excess.

Assume fixed constants \(K_\nu,K_x>0\) such that
\[
\boxed{
\nu\le K_\nu\sqrt D,\qquad x_B\le K_x\sqrt D.
}
\tag{1}
\]

The current outer-walk/fan theorem gives a family of pairwise disjoint good fan runs
\[
p^{(r)}_0,p^{(r)}_1,\ldots,p^{(r)}_{g_r}
\]
with a common diameter carrier \(a_r\) on each run and
\[
|p^{(r)}_i-a_r|=D,\qquad
|p^{(r)}_i-p^{(r)}_{i+1}|=1.
\tag{2}
\]

Let
\[
A:=\sum_r g_r
\]
be the total number of good fan contacts, and let \(R\) be the number of nonempty
good runs. The preceding theorem gives
\[
A\ge h-3\nu-x_B
\tag{3}
\]
and
\[
R\le \nu+x_B.
\tag{4}
\]

Canonical ZF gives \(h\ge\lceil D\rceil\).

A run with \(g_r\) fan contacts has \(g_r-1\) internal fan vertices. Therefore the
total number \(I\) of internal good-fan vertices is
\[
I=\sum_r(g_r-1)=A-R.
\]
Using (3)--(4),
\[
\boxed{
I\ge h-4\nu-2x_B.
}
\tag{5}
\]
Under (1),
\[
\boxed{
I\ge
\lceil D\rceil-(4K_\nu+2K_x)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{6}
\]

Thus all but \(O(\sqrt D)\) of a linear population are internal vertices of exact
\((D,D,1)\) leaf fans.

## 3. Internal fan vertices have contact degree two or three

An internal fan vertex \(p_i^{(r)}\) is a diameter leaf, so its full actual-diameter
degree is one. It already has the two positive fan contacts
\[
p_i^{(r)}p_{i-1}^{(r)},\qquad
p_i^{(r)}p_{i+1}^{(r)}.
\]

Canonical loaded-terminal geometry gives positive contact degree at most three.
Hence every internal good-fan vertex has exactly one of two types:

- **type 2:** exactly the two fan contacts;
- **type 3:** the two fan contacts plus one third unit contact.

Let
\[
T:=\#\{\text{type-3 internal fan vertices}\},
\qquad
U:=I-T.
\tag{7}
\]

For a type-3 vertex \(p\), denote its third contact neighbor by \(z(p)\).

## 4. Global unit-contact degree is at most five

### Lemma 4.1

Every source vertex has at most five unit-contact neighbors.

### Proof

Fix a source vertex \(z\). All of its contact neighbors lie on the unit circle
centered at \(z\). Any two distinct such neighbors are source points and hence are
at distance at least one. Their central angular separation at \(z\) is therefore at
least \(\pi/3\).

So \(z\) has at most six unit-contact neighbors.

If it had six, the six angular gaps around \(z\) would sum to \(2\pi\) while each
is at least \(\pi/3\). Hence every gap would equal \(\pi/3\). Consecutive neighbors
would then be at mutual distance exactly one, producing a unit contact triangle with
\(z\), forbidden by the triangle-free source hypothesis.

Therefore
\[
\boxed{\deg_C(z)\le5.}
\tag{8}
\]
QED.

This uses only unit separation and triangle-freeness. It does not use planarity,
KKT weights, or loaded status of \(z\).

## 5. Type-3 vertices force globally many distinct inward ports

The current inward-spoke theorem applies to every type-3 internal fan vertex. Write
\[
\varepsilon={1\over2D},
\qquad
\tau=\sqrt{1-\varepsilon^2},
\qquad
\delta_D={\sqrt3\,\tau-\varepsilon\over2}.
\tag{9}
\]

If \(p\) has carrier \(a\) and third neighbor \(z=z(p)\), then
\[
\boxed{
|z-a|\le D-\delta_D,
\qquad
\delta_D\longrightarrow{\sqrt3\over2}.
}
\tag{10}
\]

There are \(T\) distinct third-contact edges \(p\,z(p)\), one from each type-3
fan vertex. By Lemma 4.1, one source vertex \(z\) can be the third neighbor of at
most five such fan vertices. Therefore the number \(Z\) of distinct third-contact
neighbors satisfies
\[
\boxed{
Z\ge {T\over5}.
}
\tag{11}
\]

Thus a square-root population of type-3 fan vertices produces a square-root
population of distinct source vertices at a fixed positive inward depth from their
respective diameter carriers.

No same-carrier assumption is made in (11).

The companion theorem
research/target_c_leaf_fan_third_neighbor_inactivity_2026-10-02.md strengthens this:
every such third neighbor lies strictly inside the triangle formed by its carrier and
the two adjacent fan leaves. Hence every one of the (Z) vertices has
[
deg_H(z)=0.
]
Thus the large-(T) alternative is a population of genuinely diameter-inactive
positive-contact ports.

## 6. Type-2 vertices force a long exact stress-flat block

Along one good fan run, type-3 vertices split the type-2 internal vertices into
consecutive type-2 blocks.

If run \(r\) contains \(T_r\) type-3 internal vertices, it contains at most
\(T_r+1\) type-2 blocks. Summing over all runs, the total number \(B_2\) of nonempty
type-2 blocks obeys
\[
\boxed{
B_2\le T+R.
}
\tag{12}
\]

Hence, whenever \(U>0\), one type-2 block has length \(L\) satisfying
\[
\boxed{
L\ge {U\over T+R}
=
{I-T\over T+R}.
}
\tag{13}
\]

Every vertex of this block has contact degree exactly two. The exact KKT transport
proved in the current inward-spoke report therefore applies throughout the block.

If the block contains \(L\) consecutive internal vertices, then its \(L+1\) fan
contact edges have one common positive contact multiplier \(\lambda\), and its
\(L\) pendant diameter edges have exactly equal multipliers
\[
\boxed{
\beta_i={\lambda\over D^2}
\qquad (1\le i\le L).
}
\tag{14}
\]

Thus (13) is not merely a geometric chain-length statement: it gives an exact
stress-flat source segment.

## 7. Quantitative square-root dichotomy

Fix any constant \(\theta>0\).

If
\[
T\ge\theta\sqrt D,
\]
then (11) gives
\[
\boxed{
Z\ge{\theta\over5}\sqrt D.
}
\tag{15}
\]

Otherwise \(T<\theta\sqrt D\). Using (4), (6), and (13),
\[
L
\ge
{\lceil D\rceil-(4K_\nu+2K_x+\theta)\sqrt D
 \over
 (K_\nu+K_x+\theta)\sqrt D}.
\tag{16}
\]
Therefore
\[
\boxed{
L\ge
\left(
{1\over K_\nu+K_x+\theta}-o(1)
\right)\sqrt D.
}
\tag{17}
\]

Combining (15) and (17),
\[
\boxed{
\begin{array}{c}
\text{at least }(\theta/5)\sqrt D
\text{ distinct diameter-inactive constant-depth third-contact vertices},\\[2mm]
\text{or}\\[2mm]
\text{an exact stress-flat degree-two fan block of length }
\left({1\over K_\nu+K_x+\theta}-o(1)\right)\sqrt D.
\end{array}
}
\tag{18}
\]

One may balance the two displayed constants. Put
\[
S:=K_\nu+K_x,
\qquad
\theta_*={\sqrt{S^2+20}-S\over2},
\tag{19}
\]
so that
\[
{\theta_*\over5}={1\over S+\theta_*}.
\]
Then for every fixed
\[
0<c<{\sqrt{S^2+20}-S\over10},
\tag{20}
\]
all sufficiently large members of the branch satisfy
\[
\boxed{
Z\ge c\sqrt D
\quad\text{or}\quad
L\ge c\sqrt D.
}
\tag{21}
\]

## 8. Improvement over the one-run fork

The previous local argument selected one good fan run of length
\(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) and then split that one run at its degree-three vertices. That
gave only
\[
\Omega(D^{1/4})
\]
inward spokes or an \(\Omega(D^{1/4})\) degree-two block.

The present argument uses the linear total internal-fan population (5), not just the
longest run. The only global losses are

- \(R=O(\sqrt D)\) fan-run boundaries; and
- \(T\) actual degree-three separator vertices.

The unit-contact degree cap converts the large-\(T\) case directly into distinct
source-level inward ports, while the small-\(T\) case leaves only \(O(\sqrt D)\)
blocks across a linear number of degree-two vertices.

Hence the exponent improves sharply:
\[
\boxed{
D^{1/4}\longrightarrow D^{1/2}.
}
\tag{22}
\]

## 9. Remaining bottleneck

This still does not close C-II.

The low-\(x_B\), square-root-matching branch is now forced into one of two
source-level structures of the same square-root scale as the actual-diameter
matching:

1. **inward-port branch:** \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\) distinct diameter-inactive third-contact vertices,
   each attached by a unit edge to a diameter leaf and lying at carrier-radius
   deficit at least
   \[
   \delta_D\to\sqrt3/2;
   \]
2. **stress-flat fan branch:** an \(\Omega(\sqrt D)\)-long exact circular fan whose
   internal vertices have contact degree two, all fan-contact multipliers are equal,
   and all pendant-diameter multipliers are exactly equal to that common contact
   multiplier divided by \(D^2\).

The next closure attempts are branch-specific.

- For the inward-port branch, couple the distinct diameter-inactive ports to the
  canonical inactive attachment/interface or ball-hull capacity ledgers. Lemma 4.1
  removes arbitrary many-to-one reuse of the third neighbors, while the companion
  inactivity theorem removes loaded-diameter reuse entirely.
- For the stress-flat branch, propagate the common multiplier through the
  2-edge-connected positive-contact block or construct a finite fan release. The
  current theorem gives a segment long enough to interact with square-root-scale
  diameter localization.

No claim of a unit triangle, strict sub-\(D\) competitor, C-II closure, or Level I
closure is made.

**Disposition:** theorem/proof-only structural strengthening. No numerical search,
verifier execution, CI output, or unexecuted certificate code is used.
